tool.help:
@EARLY_VERSION@
**What it is for**
The first step in turning your eDNA data into FAIRe format is putting each of your files in the right place. The FAIRe mapper tools take several kinds of file, such as a sample sheet, OTU or ASV tables, taxonomy tables, run reports and methods text, and each kind goes in its own field. This tool looks at all the files you give it, and for each one tells you what kind of file it seems to be, why it thinks so, and which field of which tool it belongs in.
The tool makes its best guess from each file's layout, column names and values, so check each result and confirm the file's type before you go on. If you do put a file in the wrong field later, *apply mapping* will usually notice and tell you.
**How it decides**
Here is what the tool looks for in each kind of file:
- **Sample tables** have one row per sample, with columns of sample details such as dates, places, depths and volumes. Their sample names are then used to recognise the other tables. - **OTU or ASV tables** have columns of whole-number read counts, with the samples as columns (or as rows), and sequence IDs, DNA sequences or taxon names as rows. A table whose rows are taxon names holds reads summed per taxon, which is usually a final (curated) table. - **Taxonomy tables** have rank columns (Kingdom, Phylum ...) or a lineage column, and no read counts. - **Library tables** (run reports, SRA sheets) have run accessions such as SRR numbers, or library names with their sequence file names. A sample sheet that also names each sample's files is reported as a sample table that can serve as the library table too. - **Sequences in FASTA format** have a header line starting with *>* before each sequence. They are recognised by their content, whatever the file is called (.fa, .fasta, .fna, .txt or no ending). - **Text** (methods, ReadMe files), plain or as a PDF, is reported with the primer sequences it holds, if any. A PDF of scanned pages has no text the tools can read. - Crosswalks, data dictionaries, tables of values for the whole project and primer tables are recognised too.
If the tool does not recognise a file, check whether the column names are on its first row. Some sheets have a title or notes above them; when you give such a file to the other tools, set its header row.
**Output**
The result is a table with one row for each file, and one for each sheet of an Excel file. It gives the file and sheet, the kind of file, what it looks like and why, the field to give it to, and what to check or do next, such as whether an OTU table should go in as raw or final.
@HELP_INPUTS@
@HELP_FAIRE_GUIDE@
